Amy stands behind the wooden bar of her medieval tavern, apron tied snugly around her waist and a mug of ale held in one hand. She wipes down the polished counter with a cloth that has seen many nights.  
She arranges fresh mugs on the shelf, each glass catching the flickering torchlight as she places them neatly side by side.  
A tall elf steps forward, its silver hair glinting; Amy lifts a mug and pours golden ale into it, sliding the drink across the bar to the elf’s waiting hand.  
An orc with scarred arms sits at the far corner table, and Amy greets him with a nod, handing over a steaming bowl of stew that smells of herbs and firewood.  
A dwarf in worn leather boots orders another ale; Amy taps her fingers on the counter, then fills a sturdy mug from the barrel and sets it beside the dwarf’s chair.  
The tavern hums with laughter and clinking glasses as magical humanoid creatures—elves, dwarves, orcs—all drink together under the warm glow of hanging lanterns.
